---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
import BoardGallery from "../components/BoardGallery.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const parts = [
  {
    file: "neopixel.png",
    name: "An addressable RGB pixel",
    description:
      "A single pixel shown close up. For this build, buy a prewired strip or ring containing 8–16 pixels; the data travels from one to the next.",
  },
  {
    file: "breadboard.png",
    name: "Breadboard",
    description:
      "A place to plug parts together. A microcontroller needs fitted header pins to plug into it; bare holes will not make a reliable connection.",
  },
  {
    file: "jumper-wires.png",
    name: "Jumper wires",
    description:
      "Plug-in leads join the circuit. Choose male or female ends to suit your board headers, breadboard and modules.",
  },
  {
    file: "resistor-330r.png",
    name: "Data resistor",
    description:
      "For a later upgrade: this is a 330 Ω resistor. It goes in the data wire near the first LED. The quick Pico route works without one; it is worth adding when making a more robust build.",
  },
  {
    file: "capacitor.png",
    name: "Electrolytic capacitor",
    description:
      "Shape example only: buy 500–1000 µF, rated at least 6.3 V. The stripe marks the negative side. Check the value and polarity on your own part.",
  },
];
---

<Site
  title="Build your first lights"
  description="Make a light glow, change its colour and try a challenge. A quick Pico wiring route, with help for adults and experiments to discover how it works."
>
  <section class="wrap page-intro narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">YOUR FIRST MISSION</p>
    <h1>Make a light glow.</h1>
    <p class="intro">Then make it your favourite colour.</p>
    <p>
      You can try the lights on screen right now. For real lights, build with an
      adult who can help get the board and power ready. You do the choosing,
      changing and discovering.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button dark" href="https://kajlights.github.io/lightvm/">
        Try the lights on screen ↗
      </a>
      <a class="text-link" href="#parts">
        Build with a Pico →
      </a>
    </div>
    <p class="small">
      On screen: choose a lesson under <strong>Learn with lights</strong>, then
      open <strong>Try it here → Start preview</strong>.
    </p>
    <nav class="guide-links" aria-label="In this build guide">
      <a href="#parts">Get the bits</a>
      <a href="#connections">Connect</a>
      <a href="#first-pattern">First colour</a>
      <a href="#try-next">Try a challenge</a>
      <a href="#meet-the-boards">Board pictures</a>
      <a href="#make-it-reliable">Make it reliable</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap narrow" id="before-you-buy">
    <div class="testing-note">
      <p class="eyebrow">A WORKSHOP IN PROGRESS</p>
      <p>
        KajLights is in early testing, with bugs and gaps still to find. Try the
        browser first. Before buying hardware, your helper should check the{" "}
        <a href={`${base}releases/`}>firmware catalogue</a>: if there is no
        suitable download, setting up a board currently needs developer tools.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="parts">
    <p class="eyebrow">1 / GET READY TO MAKE SOMETHING</p>
    <h2>Get your bits together.</h2>
    <ul class="parts-list">
      <li>
        <strong>A Raspberry Pi Pico 2 W</strong> with LightVM already installed.
        Fitted header pins make it easier to connect.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A computer and a Micro-USB data cable.</strong> The cable powers
        the Pico and lets you talk to it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A short 5 V RGB LED strip or ring.</strong> Start with 8–16
        WS2812B-compatible pixels and fitted wires.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>
          Connecting wires and a breadboard or suitable connectors.
        </strong>{" "}
        Ask your helper to fit any parts that need soldering.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A suitable 5 V LED power supply and input capacitor.</strong>{" "}
        Your helper checks these before you switch on.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The quick Pico route sends data straight to the lights. You do not need to
      start with a level shifter or data resistor. Those become useful things to
      investigate <a href="#make-it-reliable">once it works</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="guide-links">
      <a href="#meet-the-boards">What do the boards look like?</a>
      <a href="#parts-in-pictures">What do the smaller bits look like?</a>
    </div>
    <details class="guide-detail" id="adult-setup">
      <summary>For your adult helper: buying and preparing the board</summary>
      <p>
        Use{" "}
        <a href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/products/raspberry-pi-pico-2/">
          Raspberry Pi’s retailer finder
        </a>{" "}
        and choose the exact model. A Pico W and Pico 2 W need different
        firmware. For alternatives, use the{" "}
        <a href={`${base}hardware/`}>hardware guide</a> and that model’s pinout.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Prepare this before the activity.</strong> Follow{" "}
        <a href={`${base}install/`}>Set up a device</a> if a suitable approved
        build is available. Otherwise, the{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/lightvm/blob/main/docs/pico2-w.md">
          Pico 2 W developer guide
        </a>{" "}
        requires a local build environment. For a Pico 2 W UF2, hold BOOTSEL
        while connecting USB and copy the file to the RP2350 drive. Experimental
        installs may erase earlier work.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the <a href="https://kajlights.github.io/lightvm/">LightVM editor</a>
        , connect over USB and open Configuration. Set up a WS2812B output on{" "}
        <strong>GP16</strong> with the actual pixel count, a low brightness
        limit and the strip’s colour order (commonly GRB). Save it and try a
        steady colour. Desktop Chrome or Edge supports the browser USB tools;
        use a data-capable cable.
      </p>
      <p>
        Choose <strong>5 V RGB</strong> pixels, not RGBW or a non-addressable
        strip. Use a ready-made enclosed 5 V DC adapter and wiring rated for the
        strip’s maximum current, including full white. Fit a{" "}
        <strong>500–1000 µF capacitor rated at least 6.3 V</strong> across the
        LED power input, unless already provided. Its negative stripe goes to
        ground. The{" "}
        <a href="https://learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-neopixel-uberguide/powering-neopixels">
          Adafruit power guide
        </a>{" "}
        explains sizing the supply. A breadboard is for this small starter
        circuit, not carrying the current of a long strip.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section prose" id="connections">
    <p class="eyebrow">2 / ONE WIRE CARRIES YOUR COLOUR INSTRUCTIONS</p>
    <h2>Connect the Pico straight to the lights.</h2>
    <p>
      With both power sources unplugged, connect <strong>GP16 to DIN</strong> —
      “data in” on the first LED. Follow the arrows along the strip. Your helper
      checks power, the capacitor and the shared ground before switching on.
    </p>
    <figure class="wiring-figure">
      <a
        href={`${base}assets/hardware/pico2w-direct.svg`}
        aria-label="Open the quick wiring diagram at full size"
      >
        <img
          src={`${base}assets/hardware/pico2w-direct.svg`}
          width="1280"
          height="680"
          alt="Computer powers Pico by USB. GP16 connects straight to the first LED DIN, without a resistor or level shifter. The separate 5 V supply powers the LEDs. Pico, LEDs and supply share ground. Capacitor positive goes to LED 5 V, negative to ground."
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        Quick Pico setup: data goes straight to DIN. Purple is USB, teal is
        data, red is +5 V and grey is ground. This shows connections, not
        physical pin positions.{" "}
        <a href={`${base}assets/hardware/pico2w-direct.svg`}>
          Open the quick diagram at full size ↗
        </a>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="concept-note">
      <h3>Spot the two different jobs.</h3>
      <p>
        The power wires make the lights able to shine. The data wire tells them
        which colour to show. Can you trace each path with your finger?
      </p>
    </div>
    <details class="guide-detail">
      <summary>Helper’s wiring check: pins, power and switch-on order</summary>
      <p>
        Keep the lead to the first pixel short. Use the{" "}
        <a href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/documentation/microcontrollers/pico-series.html">
          official Pico pin diagram
        </a>
        : GP16 is a signal name, <strong>not physical pin 16</strong>.
      </p>
      <table class="wiring-table">
        <caption>Quick Pico 2 W connections</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">From</th>
            <th scope="col">To</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Pico GP16</th>
            <td>First LED DIN, directly.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">External supply +5 V</th>
            <td>LED strip 5 V input.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">External supply − / GND</th>
            <td>LED ground and a Pico GND pin, joined together.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Input capacitor</th>
            <td>
              Across LED power: + to 5 V, − to ground. Check its polarity.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Pico Micro-USB</th>
            <td>Computer, for the Pico’s power and data.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        In this setup, external 5 V stays off the Pico’s power and GPIO pins.
        GPIO and 3V3 do not power the strip. Check for loose wires and shorts.
        Power the LEDs before connecting the Pico’s USB; disconnect USB before
        switching the LED supply off. Unplug both before changing connections.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="first-pattern">
    <p class="eyebrow">3 / MAKE IT YOURS</p>
    <h2>Pick a colour. Make it glow.</h2>
    <p>Once your helper has prepared and connected the board:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Open the{" "}
        <a href="https://kajlights.github.io/lightvm/">lighting editor</a>. In
        Python’s <strong>Examples…</strong> menu, choose{" "}
        <strong>color.py</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Choose your connected LED output and press{" "}
        <strong>Preview on device</strong>. Start dim. Your lights should show
        one steady colour.
      </li>
      <li>
        Open <strong>Preview settings &amp; inputs</strong>, then{" "}
        <strong>What controls this animation?</strong>. Click the colour swatch,
        pick a new colour and preview again. Can you make your favourite colour?
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <strong>You made a computer change something in the real world.</strong>{" "}
      Now give it another job.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Using the screen instead? Use <strong>Try it here → Start preview</strong>
      . You can join in with the colour and pattern challenges below.
    </p>
    <details class="guide-detail">
      <summary>Keep what you made</summary>
      <p>
        Use <strong>Save locally</strong> before closing the editor. This keeps
        your editable work on your computer. Your helper can deploy the
        animation and configure a device preset and startup behaviour when you
        want the board to keep running it. The{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/lightvm/blob/main/docs/web-editor.md">
          workspace guide
        </a>{" "}
        explains those controls.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section" id="try-next">
    <p class="eyebrow">GOT A GLOW? PICK A CHALLENGE.</p>
    <h2>Try it. Notice something. Try again.</h2>
    <div class="subjects board-anatomy">
      <article>
        <h3>Invent a signal</h3>
        <p>
          Pick colours for “come in”, “busy” and “snack time”. Can someone else
          guess your message?
        </p>
        <p>Explore: computers can use light to carry information.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Make it move</h3>
        <p>
          Try <strong>rainbow.py</strong>, or a lesson under{" "}
          <strong>Learn with lights</strong>. Predict what one change will do,
          then try it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Explore: a small change in instructions can change a whole pattern.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Be a wire detective</h3>
        <p>
          Unplug both supplies. Trace the data wire from Pico to first pixel.
          Which wires carry the power? Why are they separate?
        </p>
        <p>Explore: a circuit and a program need each other.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="why-direct-works">
    <p class="eyebrow">A QUESTION TO COME BACK TO</p>
    <h2>It works. Why add more parts?</h2>
    <p>
      Kaj regularly drives strips straight from Picos, including long strips,
      without a data resistor or level shifter. That is our quick starting
      point. He has also seen occasional flashing with an ESP32-C3; the cause
      could be signal voltage, timing or something else. It has not been pinned
      down.
    </p>
    <p>
      A circuit that works on your desk is a great start. Making it keep working
      with different wires, power supplies and LEDs is your next engineering
      challenge.
    </p>
    <details class="guide-detail">
      <summary>Why can a 3.3 V Pico talk to 5 V lights?</summary>
      <p>
        A light does not need to see exactly 5 V to recognise a “high” signal.
        Its input has a threshold, and many pixels recognise the Pico’s 3.3 V
        signal. That threshold varies between LED types and conditions. A level
        shifter gives the signal more room above it.{" "}
        <a href="https://learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-neopixel-uberguide/logic-level">
          Adafruit explores why direct drive often works
        </a>
        .
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Try explaining it:</strong> a friend can hear you in a quiet
        room. What changes when the room gets noisy? Electrical signals need
        their own kind of breathing room.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details class="guide-detail">
      <summary>
        Why can a long strip work, but a long lead cause trouble?
      </summary>
      <p>
        Each WS2812B pixel reshapes the outgoing data for the next one. The Pico
        has to reach the <strong>first pixel</strong>, rather than drive every
        pixel input at once. So a long strip is different from a long wire
        between Pico and first pixel. More pixels still need more power, and
        sending a complete frame takes longer.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Spot the difference:</strong> draw 30 closely spaced pixels. Now
        draw one pixel a long way from the Pico. Which drawing has the longer
        first data lead? The{" "}
        <a href="https://cdn-shop.adafruit.com/datasheets/WS2812B.pdf">
          Worldsemi WS2812B datasheet
        </a>{" "}
        describes the signal being reshaped at each pixel.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details class="guide-detail">
      <summary>
        Want the numbers? An optional challenge with your helper
      </summary>
      <p>
        An older{" "}
        <a href="https://cdn-shop.adafruit.com/datasheets/WS2812B.pdf">
          WS2812B datasheet
        </a>{" "}
        specifies a high input of at least{" "}
        <strong>0.7 × the LED supply voltage</strong>. At 5 V, that is 3.5 V. A
        nominal 3.3 V output is 0.2 V below that guaranteed level, even if a
        particular strip works. Other revisions and compatible LEDs have
        different limits: read the sheet for your actual parts.
      </p>
      <p>
        The gap between a driver’s guaranteed high output and a receiver’s
        required high input is one part of its <strong>noise margin</strong>.
        “It worked when we tried it” is useful evidence; a design margin helps
        it keep working when conditions change.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="glitches">
    <p class="eyebrow">SOMETHING WEIRD? YOU HAVE A MYSTERY TO SOLVE.</p>
    <h2>Be a glitch detective.</h2>
    <p>
      Pick one test. Predict what might happen. Change one thing, then check.
      Ask your helper before changing the circuit; unplug both supplies first.
    </p>
    <details class="guide-detail">
      <summary>Nothing lights up</summary>
      <p>
        Check that data reaches <strong>DIN</strong>, not DOUT, and that all
        grounds join. Check GP16, pixel count and the selected output in
        Configuration. Have your helper check power and the connections. Retry a
        steady colour at low brightness.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details class="guide-detail">
      <summary>I asked for red and got green</summary>
      <p>
        Ask your helper to check the strip’s colour order in Configuration. Many
        use GRB rather than RGB. Change only that setting and try again. A
        colour mix-up is a different clue from random flashing.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details class="guide-detail">
      <summary>The lights flicker or flash</summary>
      <p>
        Keep the same steady-colour program. Try a shorter data lead and check
        the ground connection. Then compare at a lower brightness. A
        brightness-related change is a clue to investigate power and wiring; it
        is not proof of the cause.
      </p>
      <p>
        With your helper, compare direct drive with the level-shifter circuit
        below. Improvement points towards a signal problem, but it does not
        identify voltage as the only cause. Pulse timing, electrical noise,
        power dips and software can all be involved.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details class="guide-detail">
      <summary>Helper’s investigation: power, voltage or timing?</summary>
      <p>
        Keep a short record: board, LED type, firmware build, pixel count,
        brightness, lead length and result. Compare one change at a time. Check
        the LED supply voltage at the strip under load. An oscilloscope at DIN,
        referenced to the LED’s ground, can reveal voltage levels, ringing and
        pulse widths. A multimeter cannot show those fast pulses; a logic
        analyser alone cannot establish the analogue voltage margin.
      </p>
      <p>
        Compare pulse and reset timing with the actual LED datasheet and
        firmware output settings. The ESP32-C3 flashing is an open question, not
        a confirmed voltage or timing fault. Share a reproducible example
        through the{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/lightvm/issues">
          LightVM issue tracker
        </a>
        .
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section prose" id="make-it-reliable">
    <p class="eyebrow">NEXT LEVEL / MAKE IT KEEP WORKING</p>
    <h2>Give your circuit some breathing room.</h2>
    <p>
      Once you have made your first light, compare this version with the quick
      one. What has been added? Your helper can build it with you when you want
      a setup that is less fussy about its parts and surroundings.
    </p>
    <div class="subjects board-anatomy">
      <article>
        <h3>Level shifter</h3>
        <p>
          Raises the data signal from 3.3 V to about 5 V. It adds voltage
          margin; it does not repair the wrong pulse timing.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Data resistor</h3>
        <p>
          A small resistor near the first pixel can soften signal spikes. It
          does not turn 3.3 V into 5 V or fix a weak power supply.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Power and connections</h3>
        <p>
          The input capacitor helps with sudden power changes. Sound wiring,
          common ground and a suitable supply still matter.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <figure class="wiring-figure">
      <a
        href={`${base}assets/hardware/pico2w-wiring.svg`}
        aria-label="Open the upgraded wiring diagram at full size"
      >
        <img
          src={`${base}assets/hardware/pico2w-wiring.svg`}
          width="1280"
          height="680"
          alt="Upgraded circuit: Pico GP16 to 74AHCT125 level-shifter input, its output through a resistor to LED DIN. The 5 V supply powers shifter and LEDs. All grounds join. The capacitor remains across LED power."
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        The same project, with a level shifter and data resistor added. Compare
        the teal data path with the quick diagram.{" "}
        <a href={`${base}assets/hardware/pico2w-wiring.svg`}>
          Open the upgraded diagram at full size ↗
        </a>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <details class="guide-detail">
      <summary>Helper’s upgrade parts and connections</summary>
      <p>
        Add a <strong>74AHCT125-based level-shifter module</strong> with supply
        decoupling, suitable for WS2812 signals, and a{" "}
        <strong>330–470 Ω resistor</strong>. Check whether your interface
        already includes the resistor. The capacitor from the quick circuit
        stays across LED power.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Unplug both supplies. Replace the direct wire with GP16 → module input
          → module output → resistor → LED DIN. Put the resistor near the first
          pixel.
        </li>
        <li>
          Connect the module’s 5 V supply input to the external 5 V supply and
          its GND to the shared ground.
        </li>
        <li>
          Follow the module’s pin diagram. If it exposes active-low OE (output
          enable), connect it to GND to enable the output; some modules already
          do this. Follow its instructions for any unused inputs.
        </li>
        <li>
          Check all connections, then use the same power-up order and the same
          test pattern. Compare the result.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The{" "}
        <a href="https://www.ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/sn74ahct125.pdf">
          TI AHCT125 datasheet
        </a>{" "}
        specifies a 2 V minimum high input at a 4.5–5.5 V supply. That is why
        this family can accept a 3.3 V signal and drive a higher output. A
        similarly named HC part or a slow bidirectional I²C shifter is not
        automatically a substitute.
      </p>
      <p>
        For longer or permanent installations, plan the power wiring and
        connectors for the current, including suitable protection and power
        injection where needed. Do not put a long strip’s current through
        breadboard contacts. A level shifter is not a long-distance cable
        system: shorten the first lead or use a suitable LED data
        transmitter/receiver for longer cable runs. Start with{" "}
        <a href="https://learn.adafruit.com/adafruit-neopixel-uberguide/best-practices">
          Adafruit’s wiring guidance
        </a>{" "}
        and the strip manufacturer’s instructions.
      </p>
    </details>
    <p>
      <strong>One last challenge:</strong> choose one added part. Tell someone
      what problem it helps with — and one problem it cannot fix.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section" id="meet-the-boards">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">RECOGNISE WHAT IS IN YOUR HAND</p>
        <h2>Meet the boards.</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="section-lede">
      The Pico 2 W is our example. The other photos help you recognise supported
      alternatives; use their own firmware and pin guide. A similar shape does
      not mean the same wiring. Check the model printed on the board.
    </p>
    <BoardGallery />
    <div class="subjects board-anatomy">
      <article>
        <h3>USB socket</h3>
        <p>
          The silver socket connects the board to your computer for power and
          data. Pico boards use Micro-USB; these XIAO boards use USB-C.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Microcontroller</h3>
        <p>
          The processor and memory run the program. On a Pico the main chip is
          visible; on these XIAO boards it is under the metal shield.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Pads and headers</h3>
        <p>
          The rows along the edges connect to other parts. GP16 names a
          programmable signal; GND is ground. Fitted header pins make these
          connections easier to plug in.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="parts-in-pictures">
    <p class="eyebrow">A FEW OF THE SMALLER BITS</p>
    <h2 id="parts-in-pictures">What do the components look like?</h2>
    <div class="component-gallery">
      {parts.map((part) => (
        <figure class="component-card">
          <img
            src={`${base}assets/hardware/${part.file}`}
            alt={part.name}
            width="600"
            height="500"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>
            <h3>{part.name}</h3>
            <p>{part.description}</p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="image-credit">
      Component images: Raspberry Pi Foundation, CC BY 4.0.{" "}
      <a href={`${base}image-credits/`}>Sources and licences</a>. These pictures
      help identify types of parts; the helper’s setup and upgrade notes specify
      the values to buy.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose">
    <h2>What will your next light do?</h2>
    <p>
      A secret message? A countdown? A signal from another room? Pick an idea,
      try a small version, and find out what you need to learn next.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href={`${base}learn/`}>
      More things to try →
    </a>
  </section>
</Site>
